// Ready-made design instructions for some built-in styles. Loaded into a style only if its
// instructions box is empty (prisma/seed.ts), so admin edits are never overwritten.

/** Shared blocks of the event flyer briefs (the player-card hero treatment, the flyer rules and the pro finish). */
const EVENT_HERO = `[IF PHOTO]THE HERO — the person from the uploaded photo shown TWICE, both the SAME person (exact same face, features, hair, skin tone, outfit), exactly like our player cards:
1. BEHIND: a large chest-up portrait, slightly darker, blended softly into the background with a coloured rim light (calm, confident, closed-mouth look).
2. IN FRONT: FULL BODY head to feet in a confident pose — the sharpest element of the poster, strong rim light, a real contact shadow / reflection under the feet, feet never cut off.
Pro retouch: real skin with pores and natural tone (no plastic or airbrushed look), sharp eyes, real hair strands and fabric folds. With a group photo: the group stands together as the front figure and the biggest person is the portrait behind.[/IF PHOTO][IF NO PHOTO]NO PEOPLE — no photo was uploaded, so this poster has NO people at all: no players, athletes, faces, bodies, hands, crowds or silhouettes, not even blurred or far in the background. But the poster is NOT simpler for it — it stays a DENSE, LAYERED, premium flyer like a top studio's. The hero spot is taken by a photoreal CENTREPIECE PROP, big (about 40% of the poster): a giant polished gold / chrome TROPHY (a tall cup with sweeping handles and a crown-shaped rim, the organizer logo set into its front face like an enamel medallion if a logo is given), or the organizer logo itself as a HUGE 3D metal-and-enamel emblem with real reflections — or the style's own centrepiece if the brief names one. It is dramatically lit with a hard rim light, sparks, smoke and light beams wrapping around it, and its reflection on the floor. Behind it the GIANT faded echo lettering of the HEADLINE, in front of it the designed lettering pieces, then foreground particles — every layer of this brief is kept; only the person is replaced by the prop. Never a plain text stack on an empty background. Wherever this brief mentions the hero, the portrait or the front figure, it means this centrepiece prop.[/IF NO PHOTO]`;
const EVENT_FLYER = `ANNOUNCEMENT FLYER: this is a flyer that announces what is coming — so the DATE & TIMES block is a big designed element, not a footnote; the accent line is the small "eyebrow" line that sets up the headline; the PRIZE is a hero number; and the info line is split into short rows (split it on · or / ) set as stamps, tags or icon rows. The rule / requirement line (if given) gets its OWN box with the rule note in small letter-spaced caps right under it; the entry fee (if given) gets its own big box like the prize. Words like COMING SOON, REGISTRATION OPEN or FREE ENTRY, when they appear in the given texts, become rubber-stamp / tag boxes.`;
const EVENT_FINISH = `DENSITY (the poster must never look simple): like the owner's reference flyers, EVERY area of the frame carries something — the giant echo lettering behind, the scene with real depth, the centrepiece, a second layer of graphic marks (thin HUD lines, corner brackets, small letter-spaced repeats of the given texts, stars, slashes, hex / dot textures), light streaks, sparks, smoke, shards or paper, and foreground particles slightly out of focus. At least six visible layers front to back. The information block is a DESIGNED lock-up (plates, bars, boxes, icons, dividers), never plain lines of text on a dark gradient.

ESPORTS FLAVOUR: it must feel like a pro ESPORTS organisation's announcement — LED stage screens and light bars glowing in the colours somewhere in the background, a hint of a stage truss or broadcast set, real stage haze; thin angular HUD frame lines, corner brackets, chevrons, a subtle hex or grid texture, diagonal light slashes — flat, clean and minimal (graphic shapes only, never extra words or numbers). Lettering in an esports team-logo spirit: aggressive angular cuts, wedge serifs, slanted condensed capitals with a double outline or a bevel.

PRO PHOTOSHOP FINISH (most important — it must look like a real professional club graphic made by a top designer, NOT AI)
- Photography-real: everything shot on a full-frame camera with an 85mm lens — real materials (true chrome, brushed gold, glass, concrete, paper fibres), real skin and fabric, correct hands and anatomy.
- Lighting like a pro studio: one strong key light with real falloff and true shadows, a hard coloured rim light separating the hero from the background, real contact shadows and reflections, no flat or glowing skin.
- Compositing like a top designer: clean cut-outs with natural hair edges, everything colour-matched and lit to the scene, depth layering (background → giant faded headline echo → faded logo → portrait → front hero → lettering → foreground smoke / particles slightly out of focus), soft layer-mask fades, dodge-and-burn depth, a double exposure where the portrait meets the background.
- Cinematic grade: deep but detailed blacks, controlled highlights, a subtle S-curve, one cohesive colour grade, fine film grain, gentle vignette, crisp micro-contrast, a touch of chromatic aberration and lens flare only where light hits the lens.
- Typography crafted, not generated: perfect kerning, razor-sharp edges, real materials with believable bevels, reflections and hard shadows, clean alignment on one grid; the information lines small, letter-spaced and perfectly legible.
- Restraint: effects are physically real and purposeful — no plastic CGI, no cartoon, no over-smoothing, no HDR halos, no random melted shapes, no clutter, no big empty dark areas.

TEXT RULES
- Use ONLY the texts in the text list (headline, event title, accent line, date & times, prize, info line, the collaboration label), spelled letter for letter, each written once and complete. Never invent names, prizes, dates, places, sponsors, platform or game logos, websites or handles. If an item is not given, leave its spot out.
- Logos: only the provided ones (organizer / league, collaborators, team crest), faithful and never redesigned, as premium 3D metal emblems. Never a badge copied from a shirt.
- Keep the bottom-right corner calm (brand mark goes there). No borders, frame lines around the poster or corner marks.
- VARIETY: every poster keeps this style's scene, colours, hero treatment, typography and quality, but ARRANGES the elements differently each time (hero left or right, headline block on the other margin, logo and prize in a different corner, different pose).`;

/** The showcase part of a brief, shared by every Winners-showcase style (the twins of the Photoshop player styles use it under their look). */
export const WINNERS_BODY = `CHAMPIONS SHOWCASE LAYOUT (what this poster is — follow it inside the look above)
- THE HEADLINE: the given headline big across the TOP BAND of the poster (the top quarter — it ends above the wall, never over it; smaller or on two lines if it must) as the poster's main lettering piece — built with the SAME 3D construction, materials, bevels and glow as the reference's own title (the words change, the craft does not); small lightning / laurel / star accents of the style around it.
- THE WALL OF FRAMES: the middle of the poster is a trophy-cabinet wall — a neat grid of identical bevelled display frames in the poster's metal, each lit by a small spotlight from its top edge, with a name plate along its bottom; the winners' crests and names sit in the first frames, the rest wait empty. KIXO places this wall itself (the real crests, the exact names), so when the brief says the grid area stays EMPTY, draw NOTHING there — no frames, boxes, crests or names — only the background continuing, darker and calmer, with a soft glow falling from above.
- THE BOTTOM TITLE: the given bottom title as a SMALL 3D lettering piece (about a third of the headline's size, one line, at most 9% of the height) centred along the very bottom edge in the BOTTOM BAND (it starts below the wall, never over it), in the same materials as the headline, with a small crown / laurel accent on top.
- THE EMBLEM: the league logo (if provided) as a glossy 3D emblem at the lower right corner beside the bottom title — its exact shape, letters, symbols and colours kept; no other logos, no game logos, no sponsors.
- BACKDROP: the style's own scene continuing behind everything, light streaks and rays down both sides, particles and sparks, a dark reflective floor; the footer and date lines small along the bottom.
- NO PEOPLE: there are NO people, players, athletes, faces, bodies, hands, crowds or silhouettes anywhere — not even blurred, ghosted or in duotone in the background. Where the reference shows its person, the wall of frames takes the stage instead.

PRO PHOTOSHOP FINISH (it must look like a top designer's work, NOT AI)
- Real materials and lighting: true chrome, gold, glass and enamel with physically correct reflections; real smoke, sparks and light beams; one cohesive colour grade; fine film grain; crisp micro-contrast. No plastic CGI, no cartoon, no melted shapes, no HDR halos.
- Typography crafted like a pro: perfect kerning, sharp edges, clean alignment, a strong size hierarchy — the headline and the bottom title are designed lettering pieces, never plain typed text on a gradient.
- DENSITY: every area outside the wall carries something — the scene, light streaks, the emblem, particles — at least six visible layers front to back.

TEXT RULES
- Use ONLY the texts given (headline, bottom title, league name, the date and the footer), spelled letter for letter. Never invent words, names, numbers, dates, prices, hashtags, sponsors, watermarks, game logos or legal text. If an item is not given, leave its spot out.
- The league logo appears only where this brief puts it; never redraw it as something else, never write its letters out as text elsewhere.
- Ignore the placement hints in the text list below — follow this brief.`;

// Champions styles copied from one of the owner's own champions flyers: the flyer itself — the other team's crest and
// every logo and mark blurred out — is the style's reference image (public/refs/<slug>.jpg, set by prisma/seed.ts,
// "champs_v2_flyer_refs"), and [EXACT REF] makes every poster the same design with the customer's crest and texts.
const CHAMP_FLYER_REF = `STYLE REFERENCE (most important): one reference image is labelled a STYLE reference — it is the champions flyer this style copies. Recreate THAT flyer as exactly as possible: the same layout and proportions, the same lettering styles, materials, bevels and glows, the same background scene, colours, light and effects, the same density and finish — as if the same designer made this poster. Change ONLY two things: the TEXTS (only the given texts, placed and styled like the flyer's texts — never its words, names or numbers) and the LOGOS (the given team crest stands exactly where the flyer's big blurred emblem is, at that size; the given competition logo where the notes below put it — otherwise nothing there). When a player photo is given, the player is added as the notes below say; nothing else moves. The grey blurred areas in the reference are its removed crest, logos and marks: never reconstruct, trace or imitate what was there, and never paint a blur or a grey patch — a blurred spot that gets nothing from the notes below simply continues the scene around it. Never copy the flyer's names, words, numbers, emblems, mascots, characters or logos. The notes below are the map of that flyer (what each part is and what goes there); where they and the flyer differ in look, the flyer wins. The only changes to the look are the owner's standard for champions posters: MORE GLOW, MORE PARTICLES, the team crest once more, giant and faded, in the background, and the SQUAD NAMES as designed metallic lettering instead of the flyer's plain names (see EXTRA GLOW & PARTICLES and the USER CHOICES).`;

